Serverless = you write the function, the cloud runs it only when needed, and you pay only for the time it runs.

“Serverless” doesn’t mean there are no servers. There are servers, but someone else manages them.

Analogy
                     Own Car (Traditional Server)	                          Taxi / Uber (Serverless)	
Who buys it?	You	Nobody, it’s shared
Maintenance	You (oil, tires, insurance)	The company
Cost when parked	Still paying	Zero
Rush hour	Only one car	More taxis appear automatically


How it works
User clicks → API Gateway → Function (runs) → Database → Response
                               ↑
                    Starts only on request,
                    shuts down after finishing
Step by step:
	1. An event happens (an HTTP call, a file upload, a Kafka message, a timer).
	2. The cloud spins up your function.
	3. The function does its one job.
	4. The function shuts down.
	5. You are billed only for those milliseconds.

Building blocks
	• FaaS (Function as a Service): your code, e.g. AWS Lambda, Azure Functions, Google Cloud Functions
	• BaaS (Backend as a Service): managed DB, auth, storage, e.g. DynamoDB, Cognito, S3
	• API Gateway: the front door that routes requests to functions
	• Event sources: what triggers the function (HTTP, S3 upload, SQS/Kafka, cron)

Why companies love it
	• No server management: no patching, no OS, no capacity planning
	• Auto-scaling: 1 request or 1 million, the cloud handles it
	• Pay per use: idle time costs nothing
	• Faster delivery: developers focus on business logic

The catch
	• Cold start: the first request after idle is slower because the function has to boot. This is especially noticeable with Java and Spring Boot.
	• Time limits: Lambda caps at 15 minutes per run, so no long jobs.
	• Stateless: a function can’t remember anything between runs. State must live in a DB or cache.
	• Vendor lock-in: you get tied to one cloud’s ecosystem.
	• Hard to debug locally: distributed and event-driven flows are harder to trace.
	• Cost surprise: at constant heavy traffic, always-on servers can be cheaper.

Trade Off
Use it for:
	• Spiky or unpredictable traffic
	• Event-driven work (image resize, email send, file processing)
	• Scheduled jobs and webhooks
	• MVPs and startups
Avoid it for:
	• Long-running processes
	• Ultra low-latency needs (trading, gaming)
	• Steady, high, predictable load
	• Heavy in-memory or stateful workloads

Example: image upload pipeline
User uploads photo → S3 bucket
                        │ (event)
                        ▼
                 Lambda: resize image
                                          │
          ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
          ▼                                                           ▼
   Save thumbnail to S3     Save metadata to DynamoDB
                                       │
                                      ▼
                           Lambda: notify user (SNS)
There are no servers running while nobody uploads. At 10,000 uploads in a minute, Lambda scales out automatically.

Java / Spring Boot angle
The biggest issue is cold start, because the JVM is slow to boot. The fixes for it:
	• AWS Lambda SnapStart: snapshots the initialized JVM so restores are fast
	• Spring Cloud Function: write plain Function<I, O> beans that deploy to any cloud
	• Keep functions small, single-purpose, and with few dependencies
	• Use provisioned concurrency for latency-critical paths

Rule of thumb: Start serverless for speed and cost. Move to containers when load becomes steady and predictable.






